  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    Wane☽ Oncea is a trade settlement in the southern reaches of Nightfall Shelf, the labyrinthine tunnel-colony carved into the Berg's interior. It comprises three interconnected caverns and an indeterminate depth of tunnels below them, extending past multiple collapse-points to a deep chamber called ☽'dwell Teneanemus~, which the Nightshelf names the Scar-Keep. The settlement was carved during the Great Thaw — a period the chronicles remember as optimism and the ice remembers as starvation — and has changed hands three times since. It has survived two corruption blooms. It functions today as a trade hub, a diplomatic venue mandated by the Flipper Accord, and — in Nightshelf fable tradition — as proof that what is taken in darkness cannot be kept.
    
    The entrance smells of char and old blood, worked into the stone beyond any scrubbing. The bioluminescent moss that lines the caverns flickers erratically — bright in some patches, nearly extinguished in others. Trade continues regardless.
    
    ## Successive Holders
    
    The architectural record of Wane☽ Oncea is legible in its walls, each era overwriting the last imperfectly.
    
    | **Period** | **Holder** | **Marks Left** |
    |---|---|---|
    | Founding (Great Thaw) | ☽'ofund' Guild, the Nightshelf artisan-trade guild | Fire-vaults carved with precision; signature crystal-work in the uppermost chamber. Guild-marks have been deliberately chipped away — by whom and when is unrecorded. |
    | Expansion | The Icebound Exchange, the merchant guild now nominally controlling Aurora Stack's trade routes | Crude downward tunnels, efficiently dug. The sources do not romanticize the labor conditions. |
    | Third tenure | The Wake-Singers, the orca shaman-cult devoted to waking their dead god | Spiraling glyphs burned into walls; offerings of bone and frozen fish left in alcoves that present-day merchants refuse to touch. |
    
    The transfer mechanisms — whether conquest, abandonment, or negotiation — are poorly documented for the first two transitions. The Wake-Singers' presence in a penguin trade settlement deep inside the Berg raises questions that no chronicle I have reviewed adequately addresses. That orca shamans held territory this far into Nightshelf infrastructure, and that the infrastructure survived their tenure, suggests arrangements more complex than the raid-and-retreat narrative typically applied to Wake-Singer activity.
    
    ## The Deep Tunnels and Jurisdictional Gap
    
    Below the trade caverns, tunnels descend at least three fathoms past collapse-points to ☽'dwell Teneanemus~. Jurisdiction over this deeper sector has been contested between Qingfu'spire, an Aurora Stack governance body, and the Shadow Covens, Nightshelf occult practitioners, since before the Orca Incursion — a territorial dispute so old that neither claimant has exercised effective control within living memory. The tunnels below the trade levels remain functionally unmonitored: a gap that chronicle sources confirm has been exploited by practitioners willing to make the descent, including the beacon-keeper Lianhinglou, who entered the Scar-Keep during a resource crisis and returned bearing knowledge and crystalline scars in roughly equal measure.
    
    ## The Corruption Blooms and the Cleansing
    
    The canonical record states that corruption bloomed from the ice at Wane☽ Oncea twice. The first bloom is attested only by implication — no chronicle in my possession describes it directly. The second is well documented.
    
    The second bloom manifested as cold-burn discoloration spreading through the trade-stall walls, whisper-echoes in the southern tunnels, and ice-memory contamination: touching the stone produced visions of events that never occurred. The taint extended beyond the settlement's three caverns into the surrounding tunnel network and down into the contested depths. Merchants and ☽'ofund' Guild operatives worked within the corruption for years. Some profited from it — contact with presences in the tainted walls, information no living source could otherwise provide. The merchant ☽'aeteraea is named in chronicle accounts as having built trade networks inside the wrongness itself.
    
    The cleansing was triggered when Fissure-Dealer ☽'Sanctteinvaus, a Nightshelf practitioner, carried the grimoire ☽'numu' Witness into the settlement during a fire-core escort. The tome's activation drew fire from every fire-core, brazier, and crystal in range. The cold-burn was burned from the walls. The whisper-echoes went silent. The ice-memory was scoured to bedrock. The cost fell on those who had carried or profited from the corruption — ☽'aeteraea's networks were cauterized, and ☽'Sanctteinvaus herself was permanently altered, her flipper frozen in the shape of the tome's grip. The grimoire now rests in the uppermost chamber, in the old ☽'ofund' crystal-work where the guild-marks were chipped away. The cavern where the corruption was thickest is now its thinnest.
    
    ## The Directrix Mirror and the Calving
    
    The Directrix Mirror, a navigational artifact, was stored in a vault behind three layers of ice-lock, guarded by rotation but rarely opened. Local accounts held that the Mirror ceased showing true directions after the Profun∴lapsum, a seismic collapse that flooded Wane☽ Oncea's deep mines. No one confirmed this — the Mirror was not the sort of thing people checked voluntarily.
    
    The Calving that tore open the Berg's eastern face sent floodwaters through the archive chambers, submerging stored records and trade-maps beneath meltwater that froze unevenly. The Mirror was retrieved from the flood damage with a jagged fissure across its face. It was returned to its vault — functional, according to those who examined it, but untouched since.
    
    ## Present Function and Cultural Status
    
    Under the Flipper Accord, Wane☽ Oncea serves as the mandated venue for face-to-face resolution of charter disputes — a diplomatic function that requires surface-dwellers to descend into the Nightshelf to honor their obligations, or find reasons not to. Merchants from Nightfall Shelf haul fire-cores and salvage through the main passage. Tally-marks carved into the walls to track shift rotations go back decades; some are official, most are not.
    
    Nightshelf fable tradition names Wane☽ Oncea as a gathering place for practitioners of Shawl☽ Sun, a fire-memory magic associated with the cautionary tale of ☽'slew Nisaemesius~, a colony destroyed when its practitioners burned too deep.
    
    Kaela, who tends the north-vault's braziers and has not missed a rotation in seven winters, still leaves two cups of fire-tea on the old ☽'ofund' ledger each morning — one for the day's work, one for whoever was here before the wars. The earlier accounts state that no one drank the second cup. The current accounts say someone does. Kaela will not say who.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
